Ladybird lays out an inline formatting context containing only atomic
boxes and whitespace from its paragraph direction. In a right-to-left
context, item edges advance from the right, and final line placement
maps each logical offset to its visual position. This keeps asymmetric
padding and margins on their physical sides, including wrapped inline
parents. Paragraphs with plaintext or mixed-text content are excluded.

Blink represents atomic inline boxes as object replacement characters
in InlineItemsBuilderTemplate::AppendAtomicInline. Its line builder
uses their resolved embedding levels in LogicalLineBuilder::BidiReorder.
Gecko adds object substitutes in nsBidiPresUtils::TraverseFrames, and
nsBidiPresUtils::ReorderFrames orders their frames. WebKit builds the
same neutral characters in the paragraph prepared by its inline
builder. InlineItemsBuilder::breakAndComputeBidiLevels resolves the
levels, and computedVisualOrder in InlineLineBuilder orders runs.

All three resolve paragraph-wide bidi, including text around atomic
boxes. Ladybird implements the neutral-only case here: Atomic boxes
and whitespace inherit the paragraph direction, without interpreting
the text inside each atomic box as part of its containing paragraph.
Mixed text and atomic boxes still need paragraph-wide bidi resolution.

Chrome places the first 80px child at x=140 in the 220px RTL scroller,
followed by the 500px child at x=-360 and the 300px child at x=-660.
Controls cover asymmetric edges, wrapping, whitespace, changed
paragraph direction, appended children, and plaintext paragraphs.
